Translation patch: the elicitation schema builders of
crates/rmcp/src/model/elicitation_schema.rs, so that X29 enum_titles, X32
EnumSchemaBuilder<SingleSelect>::with_default, X35-X37 EnumSchemaBuilder<MultiSelect>::
{with_default, min_items, max_items}, X33/X38 EnumSchemaBuilder<..>::build, X40
ElicitationSchema::new and X74/X75 ElicitationSchemaBuilder::{build, build_unchecked} translate
and build with exact external models (human decision XD1, 2026-11-28: adopted as drafted, 11
hunks, the private opaque module prim with titles_mismatch_message and sorted_keys; linkage
patch-08 for these ten targets, X75 because it calls X74). The other 65 targets of X01-X75
translate unmodified and are not touched (linkage ORIGINAL, certified by
scripts/check-original-linkage.sh, human decision XD2).

Applies to: the copy of upstream origin/main 7caec762799bece6fba38e4298b8ca7db8bac12c after
patches 01-07 (scripts/apply-patches.sh, patch -p1 -F 0, only to the copy in .llbc/work/; the
real source is never patched). File: crates/rmcp/src/model/elicitation_schema.rs only (blob
929b446 upstream; ca78a8d after patches 01-07, where 02-phase2-tests.patch appends the XD tests
at the end of mod tests and no other patch touches the file; 3c55487 after this patch). Every
hunk lies before mod tests, so it applies at its recorded lines. Generated with git diff from
the patch-01..07 copy, formatted with rustfmt of nightly-2026-09-17 and the repository
rustfmt.toml. Toolchain measured: Aeneas nightly-2026.11.24-fb44cb4, Charon 0.1.324, Rust
nightly-2026-09-17.

Not part of this patch (they stay in the repository): the Charon settings in
crates/rmcp/Cargo.toml (73 start_from entries for X01-X75; the opaque entry
rmcp::model::elicitation_schema::prim), TARGETS and PATCH08_TARGETS of
scripts/aeneas-translate.sh, the patch08 set of scripts/check-original-linkage.sh, the
translation pin, and the external models of formal/trusted/Rmcp/FunsExternal.lean (section
"Elicitation schema builders") and TypesExternal.lean (BTreeMap; F64/F32, human decision XD3).

Change (non-test lines only; no test is added or changed):
 prim  New private module `prim` before `mod tests`, opaque in the translation and modelled in
       Lean, each the original expression with the same arguments: titles_mismatch_message
       (the `format!` of enum_titles, same format string and arguments) and sorted_keys
       (`properties.keys().cloned().collect()` into a Vec<String>).
 E1  X29 enum_titles: `prim::titles_mismatch_message(self.enum_values.len(), titles.len())`.
 E2  X32 with_default (single): the error literal's `.to_string()` becomes `.to_owned()`.
 E3  X35 with_default (multi): the `for` loop with an early `return Err(..)` becomes
     `if !default_values.iter().all(|value| self.enum_values.contains(value)) { return Err(..) }`;
     the three error literals use `.to_owned()`.
 E8  X33/X38 EnumSchemaBuilder<SingleSelect|MultiSelect>::build, titled branch: the
     `self.enum_titles.into_iter().zip(self.enum_values).map(|(title, const_)| ConstTitle {
     const_, title }).collect()` becomes `const_titles(self.enum_titles, self.enum_values)`, a new
     private (translated, not opaque) function that loops `for title in titles { match
     values.next() { Some(const_) => out.push(ConstTitle { const_, title }), None => break } }`.
 E4  X36 min_items, E5 X37 max_items: the two error literals each use `.to_owned()`.
 E6  X40 ElicitationSchema::new: `prim::sorted_keys(&properties)`.
 E7  X74 ElicitationSchemaBuilder::build: `names = prim::sorted_keys(&self.properties)` first;
     the validation becomes `!self.required.is_empty() && !self.required.iter().all(|f|
     names.contains(f))`; the property order is `Some(names)`. (X75 build_unchecked is
     unchanged; it calls X74.)

Why each hunk is needed (measured 2026-11-28 in a scratch copy, each target alone):
 prim, E6, E7  `m.keys().cloned().collect()` on a BTreeMap: "[Error] Internal error" at the
     Iterator trait declaration (core/src/iter/traits/iterator.rs 42), then "Fatal error:
     exception Aeneas.Errors.CFailure" (SymbolicToPureTypes.translate_fun_sigs).
 E7  the loop over `&self.required` with `return Err` inside: "[Error] Early returns inside of
     loops are not supported yet". Any loop or closure that calls `self.properties.contains_key`
     fails because the map's value type holds a `Cow<'static, str>`: a `for` with a flag
     ("unsupported nested borrows"), a `while` with an index ("Not implemented"),
     `.iter().all(|f| self.properties.contains_key(f))` with or without a `prim::contains_key`
     wrapper ("Internal error" in the closure's call_mut). Validating against the key list (a
     Vec<String>) avoids the map inside the loop.
 E1, prim  `format!` translates to `alloc::fmt::format` of a `core::fmt::Arguments`, which the
     Aeneas library defines as `Unit`: no exact model exists.
 E2-E5  `"..".to_string()` translates to the blanket `ToString::to_string` through
     `Display for str`, whose `core::fmt::Formatter` is opaque in the Aeneas library: no exact
     model exists. `str::to_owned` has a reviewed model (patch 03).
 E8  Aeneas translates the chain (no error) but generates `Iterator` instances for the std
     adapters `Map<I, F>` and `Zip<A, B>` with fields `zip`, `map`, `collect`, `all`, `any`,
     which the Iterator structure of the Aeneas library does not have (Aeneas warns "could not
     find the information for item 'zip'"); `lake build` then fails with "`zip` is not a field
     of structure `core.iter.traits.iterator.Iterator`" (10 errors in Rmcp.Funs).
 E3  the `for` loop with an early return: "[Error] Internal error, please file an issue"
     (Interp.ml 686; the body becomes `sorry`).
No upstream Charon or Aeneas issue has been filed; the reproducers and draft issue texts are
recorded in formal/trusted/issues/aeneas-elicitation-schema.md.

Why the behaviour is unchanged: every value returned is the same. The prim functions are the
original expressions. `str::to_string` and `str::to_owned` both return a `String` with the bytes
of the literal (`ToString` for `str` is specialised to `String::from`, i.e. `to_owned`).
`iter().all(p)` stops at the first element for which `p` is false, where the loop returned, and
`contains` has no side effect. In E7 `contains_key(f)` on the map and `contains(f)` on the list
of all its keys agree for every `f` (`Ord for String` agrees with `Eq`), and the key list is the
value the original computed after the check; computing it first adds the allocation of that
list on the error path. In Rust this changes no returned value (the list is dropped). In the Lean
model it is visible: prim::sorted_keys is `ExtModel.collectVec 24 ..`, which is `fail panic`
when 24 * len bytes exceed isize::MAX, so the translated X74 returns the validation `Err` only
under the premise `24 * properties.entries.length <= isize::MAX` (unreachable for a real Rust
map: its keys are 24-byte `String`s that already exist in memory), and specs of the `Err` path
must state it. In E8 the loop pairs the i-th title with the i-th value
and stops when either list ends, as `Zip::next` (which calls the titles' `next` first and drops
nothing observable); the result holds the same elements in the same order, but its capacity
differs (`one_of`/`any_of` are `pub` Vec fields: for
`EnumSchema::builder(vec!["v"]).enum_titles(vec!["t"]).unwrap().build()` it is 4 patched, by
`Vec::push` growth, and 1 upstream, by the exact `TrustedLen` collect). The capacity is visible
only through `Vec::capacity` and memory use, and is not modelled (a Lean `Vec` is its list of
elements). `a && b` is the nested `if`; for an empty `required`,
`all` is true anyway.

